    },
    {
      "type": "table_legend",
      "continuation": false,
      "heading_level": null,
      "md_text": "EA = Effective Address\n\nDn = Data Register\n\nAn = Address Register\n\n( ) = Contents of\n\nPC = Program Counter\n\nd₈ = 8-Bit Offset (Displacement)\n\nd₁₆ = 16-Bit Offset (Displacement)\n\nN = 1 for byte, 2 for word, and 4 for long word. If An is the stack pointer and the operand size is byte, N = 2 to keep the stack pointer on a word boundary.\n\n← = Replaces\n\nXn = Address or Data Register used as Index Register\n\nSR = Status Register\n\nUSP = User Stack Pointer\n\nSSP = Supervisor Stack Pointer\n\nCP = Program Counter\n\nVBR = Vector Base Register",
      "bbox": [
        530,
        1278,
        1702,
        1856
      ],
      "segment_id": "page_0019_seg_005"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "continuation": false,
      "heading_level": 2,
      "md_text": "2.3 DATA ORGANIZATION IN REGISTERS",
      "bbox": [
        192,
        1936,
        1183,
        1978
      ],
      "segment_id": "page_0019_seg_006"
    },
    {
      "type": "prose",
      "continuation": false,
      "heading_level": null,
      "md_text": "The eight data registers support data operands of 1, 8, 16, or 32 bits. The seven address registers and the active stack pointer support address operands of 32 bits.",
      "bbox": [
        192,
        2011,
        1800,
        2095
      ],
      "segment_id": "page_0019_seg_007"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "continuation": false,
      "heading_level": 3,
      "md_text": "2.3.1 Data Registers",
      "bbox": [
        193,
        2160,
        665,
        2207
      ],
      "segment_id": "page_0019_seg_008"
    },
    {
      "type": "prose",
      "continuation": false,
      "heading_level": null,
      "md_text": "Each data register is 32 bits wide. Byte operands occupy the low-order 8 bits, word operands the low-order 16 bits, and long-word operands, the entire 32 bits. The least significant bit is addressed as bit zero; the most significant bit is addressed as bit 31.",
      "bbox": [
        193,
        2234,
        1805,
        2369
      ],
      "segment_id": "page_0019_seg_009"
    },
